<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ānanda</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">5th–4th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a> · Also known as Ananda</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ānanda was the primary attendant of the Buddha and one of his ten principal disciples, remembered for his memory. In early Buddhist texts he was the Buddha's first cousin, and he became attendant twenty years into the Buddha's ministry, accompanying him for the rest of his life. He requested on behalf of Mahāpajāpati Gotamī that women be allowed to be ordained, and he was with the Buddha in his last year. Tradition holds that he became enlightened just before the First Council, where he recited the Buddha's discourses and was also reproved by Mahākassapa. Scholars are skeptical about the historicity of many events of his life. He is said to have died 20 years after the Buddha.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ananda.jpg" alt="Ānanda" width="330" height="440" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Daderot · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Head_of_the_Disciple_Ananda,_Hebei_province,_Fengfeng,_northern_Xiangtangshan_Cave_Temples,_South_Cave,_Northern_Qi_dynasty,_550-577_AD,_limestone_with_traces_of_pigment_-_Freer_Gallery_of_Art_-_DSC05697.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
